The mechanics, verified:

```js
// SYNC: throw unwinds until caught
try {
  null.x;                          // TypeError: cannot read properties of null
} catch (e) {
  e.name;      // 'TypeError'     — branch on error TYPE
  e.message;   // human detail
  e.stack;     // stack trace string (best-effort, not a standard)
} finally {
  // ALWAYS runs — verified: even a return in try completes finally first
}

// THE TYPE HIERARCHY (branch on it):
// null.x                    → TypeError       ✓ (verified)
// undeclaredVariable        → ReferenceError  ✓ (verified)
// JSON.parse('{bad')        → SyntaxError     ✓ (verified)

// ASYNC — the boundary that catches juniors:
try {
  fetch('/api').then(d => d.missing);   // rejection happens LATER
} catch (e) {
  // NEVER REACHED — the sync try/catch already exited
  // the error occurs in a future microtask, after this block finished
}
// CORRECT: catch in the async chain
fetch('/api').catch(e => ...);
// or with await:
try { const d = await fetch('/api'); }   // rejection → THIS catch ✓
catch (e) { ... }
```

The two events for what escaped (the production layer):
- window.onerror / 'error' event — sync errors that nothing caught
- window 'unhandledrejection' — Promise rejections with no .catch
These are where error-reporting SDKs (Sentry et al.) hook in — an uncaught async error does NOT crash the page (the event loop continues), it just never reaches your handler unless you subscribe to these events.

Design guidance (the judgment layer): catch what you can HANDLE — at the boundary where recovery is possible (retry, fallback UI, user message). Not everywhere (blanket try/catch swallows bugs), not nowhere (users see blank screens). Custom errors: class HttpError extends Error { constructor(status) { super(...); this.status = status; } } — the pattern that lets catch blocks branch on domain categories.
